# Project structure

**Primary source:** Gradle with Kotlin DSL and version catalogs
(`gradle/libs.versions.toml`) — Android's own current default for
dependency and plugin management (`developer.android.com/build/migrate-to-catalogs`).

**Community layer:** none needed for the base — version catalogs are a
Gradle/Android Studio-native mechanism. Convention plugins (an included
`build-logic` build) are an optional modularization layer once module
count and team size justify it; this pattern is documented by Google
but not enforced by tooling the way Hilt or Room are.

## Default layout (single module, day 0)

```text
<app>/
  gradle/libs.versions.toml   # single source of truth for dependency + plugin versions
  app/
    build.gradle.kts
    src/main/kotlin/<package>/
      MainActivity.kt
      core/
        logging/
        network/
        auth/
        analytics/
        persistence/
        navigation/
        di/                    # Hilt modules
      feature/
        login/
        <feature>/
      designsystem/
    src/test/kotlin/...        # JUnit4 + MockK + Turbine + Roborazzi
    src/androidTest/kotlin/... # instrumented critical-journey suite
```

## When to introduce convention plugins / build-logic

Introduce an included `build-logic` build (via an ADR, per
`android-adr.mdc`) when **any** of:

- The app has grown past ~3 feature modules and copy-pasted
  `build.gradle.kts` boilerplate is visibly drifting between modules
- More than 2 engineers are regularly touching shared build configuration
- A feature module needs to be built/tested in isolation for CI
  parallelization

**Do not** use `buildSrc` for this — it invalidates the entire build
cache on any change. Use an included build (`build-logic`) so build-logic
changes don't force a full project recompilation.

## Module boundary rules

- **`core/*`** modules never import from **`feature/*`**. If a core
  service needs feature-specific behavior, invert the dependency
  (interface in core, implementation in the feature).
- **`feature/<A>`** does not depend on **`feature/<B>`** directly. Shared
  behavior between features moves to `core/` or `designsystem/`.
- **`app`** is the only module allowed to wire feature entry points
  together (via Hilt's `@AndroidEntryPoint`/`@HiltAndroidApp` and the
  navigation graph).
- Prefer `implementation` over `api` for module dependencies to minimize
  recompilation of downstream modules when an internal detail changes.
- Changing this graph (new Gradle module, new convention plugin) once
  established requires an ADR (`android-adr.mdc` trigger #8).

## Dependency pinning

- Every dependency and plugin version lives in `gradle/libs.versions.toml`
  — never a hardcoded version string in a module's `build.gradle.kts`.
- **KSP, not KAPT**, for annotation processing (Hilt, Room) — KAPT is
  deprecated and meaningfully slower.
- Review and bump dependency versions deliberately; do not enable
  automatic dependency updates unreviewed in CI.

